{"ok": true, "summary": "Cause: the dynamic regex parser (src/dynpy/rt_text.rs) registered each `(?P<name>...)` group in its names list without checking for a duplicate name, so patterns CPython rejects compiled silently. The typed build already refuses named-group syntax and falls back to the dynamic build.

Change: in `RP::group`, right after reading a named group's name, look the name up in the already-registered names and, when found, fail with CPython's exact error: `redefinition of group name '<name>' as group <ngroups+1>; was group <old> at position <name start>` (position is the raw char index where the name begins, matching CPython for nested/repeated/verbose/non-ASCII cases). The check runs before the group number is consumed, so the reported numbers match CPython.

Tested: `run_check` passes (normal and BREVI_DYN=1, output and exit code identical to python3, 40/40 guard programs still typed); verified via try_python that longer names, unnamed/nested/repeated/alternated surrounding groups, non-ASCII names, verbose mode and re.match/re.findall variants all print exactly what CPython 3.13 prints. Added tests/lib/review_m06_re_duplicate_group.py with its python3-derived .expected (the original program plus nearby cases) and changelog.d/review-m06.md."}
